                'model_answer' => 'The elimination method for solving systems of linear equations involves the following steps:\n\n1. Write both equations in standard form (ax + by = c).\n2. Multiply one or both equations by constants to make the coefficients of one variable (either x or y) equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n3. Add the equations to eliminate one variable.\n4. Solve for the remaining variable.\n5. Substitute this value back into either original equation to find the other variable.\n6. Check the solution in both original equations.\n\nExample: Solve the system\n2x + 3y = 7\n4x - 5y = 3\n\nStep 1: Both equations are already in standard form.\nStep 2: Multiply the first equation by 2 to get 4x + 6y = 14.\nStep 3: Add this to the second equation: (4x + 6y) + (4x - 5y) = 14 + 3\n8x + y = 17\nStep 4: Solve for x: x = (17 - y)/8\nStep 5: Substitute into the first equation: 2((17 - y)/8) + 3y = 7\nSimplify: (17 - y)/4 + 3y = 7\n17 - y + 12y = 28\n11y = 11\ny = 1\nThen x = (17 - 1)/8 = 2\nStep 6: Check: 2(2) + 3(1) = 7 ✓ and 4(2) - 5(1) = 3 ✓\nSolution: x = 2, y = 1',
