            [
                'question_text' => 'Explain the concept of simple harmonic motion and derive the equation for the period of a simple pendulum. Discuss the assumptions made in this derivation.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Simple Harmonic Motion (SHM) is a type of periodic motion where the restoring force is directly proportional to the displacement from the equilibrium position and acts in the opposite direction to the displacement.\n\nMathematically, this is expressed as F = -kx, where F is the restoring force, k is the spring constant, and x is the displacement from equilibrium. This leads to the differential equation d²x/dt² = -(k/m)x, where m is the mass of the object.\n\nThe solution to this equation is x = A cos(ωt + φ), where A is the amplitude, ω = √(k/m) is the angular frequency, and φ is the phase constant.\n\nFor a simple pendulum of length L, the restoring force is F = -mg sin θ, where θ is the angle from the vertical. For small angles, sin θ ≈ θ, so F ≈ -mgθ. Since the arc length s = Lθ, we have F ≈ -mg(s/L) = -(mg/L)s.\n\nComparing with F = -kx, we see that k = mg/L for a simple pendulum. The period is given by T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(L/g).\n\nAssumptions made in this derivation:\n1. The angle of oscillation is small (sin θ ≈ θ).\n2. The pendulum bob is a point mass.\n3. The string is massless and inextensible.\n4. There is no air resistance or friction.\n5. The gravitational field is uniform.',
