                'question_text' => 'Explain the concept of hybridization in molecular orbital theory and discuss the hybridization in ethene (C₂H₄) with reference to its structure and bonding.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Hybridization is the mixing of atomic orbitals to form new hybrid orbitals suitable for the pairing of electrons to form chemical bonds. It explains the observed molecular geometries of many molecules that cannot be explained by simple valence bond theory.\n\nIn ethene (C₂H₄), each carbon atom undergoes sp² hybridization. In this process, one s orbital and two p orbitals of the carbon atom mix to form three sp² hybrid orbitals. These hybrid orbitals lie in a plane and are oriented at 120° to each other.\n\nEach carbon atom in ethene has three sp² hybrid orbitals and one unhybridized p orbital. The sp² hybrid orbitals are used to form sigma (σ) bonds: one σ bond between the two carbon atoms and two σ bonds with hydrogen atoms. The unhybridized p orbitals on each carbon atom overlap laterally to form a pi (π) bond.\n\nThis results in a double bond between the carbon atoms, consisting of one σ bond and one π bond. The π bond restricts rotation around the C-C axis, making the molecule planar. The bond angle between the sp² hybrid orbitals is approximately 120°, which is observed in the H-C-H and H-C-C bond angles in ethene.\n\nThe hybridization explains the planar structure of ethene, the restricted rotation around the C=C bond, and the observed bond angles and bond lengths.',
