        'questions' => [
            [
                'question_text' => 'In a dihybrid cross between two heterozygous parents (AaBb × AaBb), what is the probability of obtaining an offspring with the genotype aabb?',
                'options' => [
                    '1/4',
                    '1/8',
                    '1/16',
                    '3/16'
                ],
                'correct_answer' => '1/16',
                'explanation' => 'For each gene, the probability of getting the recessive allele from a heterozygous parent is 1/2. So the probability of getting a from the first parent is 1/2, and from the second parent is also 1/2. Similarly for b. Therefore, the probability of aabb is (1/2 × 1/2) × (1/2 × 1/2) = 1/4 × 1/4 = 1/16.',
                'order' => 1
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'Explain the process of DNA replication, including the enzymes involved and the mechanisms that ensure accuracy. Discuss the significance of semiconservative replication.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'DNA replication is the process by which DNA makes a copy of itself during cell division. It is semiconservative, meaning that each new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly synthesized strand.\n\nThe process begins with the enzyme helicase breaking the hydrogen bonds between the complementary base pairs, unwinding the DNA double helix and creating a replication fork. Single-strand binding proteins attach to the separated strands to prevent them from reannealing.\n\nDNA polymerase III then adds nucleotides to the growing DNA strand, but it can only add nucleotides in the 5\'→3\' direction. This results in continuous synthesis on the leading strand (in the same direction as the replication fork movement) and discontinuous synthesis on the lagging strand (in the opposite direction).\n\nOn the lagging strand, DNA polymerase III synthesizes short fragments called Okazaki fragments. RNA primase adds RNA primers to provide a 3\' end for DNA polymerase III to start synthesis. DNA polymerase I removes the RNA primers and replaces them with DNA. Finally, DNA ligase joins the Okazaki fragments together.\n\nAccuracy is ensured through several mechanisms:\n1. Base-pairing specificity (A with T, G with C)\n2. Proofreading function of DNA polymerase III, which checks each nucleotide after it is added\n3. Mismatch repair systems that correct errors after replication\n\nThe semiconservative nature of DNA replication is significant because it ensures that each new cell receives a DNA molecule containing one strand with the original genetic information, reducing the chance of errors being passed on. It was confirmed by the Meselson-Stahl experiment in 1958, which distinguished it from conservative and dispersive models of replication.',
                'order' => 2
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'A man with blood type A marries a woman with blood type B. They have a child with blood type O. What are the genotypes of the parents?',
                'options' => [
                    'AA and BB',
                    'AO and BO',
                    'AA and BO',
                    'AO and BB'
                ],
                'correct_answer' => 'AO and BO',
                'explanation' => 'For a child to have blood type O (genotype OO), both parents must carry the O allele. Since the father has blood type A, his genotype must be AO (not AA). Similarly, the mother with blood type B must have genotype BO (not BB).',
                'order' => 3
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'What is the function of telomerase in eukaryotic cells?',
                'options' => [
                    'It catalyzes the addition of nucleotides to the 3\' end of DNA during replication',
                    'It adds repetitive sequences to the ends of chromosomes to prevent loss of genetic information',
                    'It removes RNA primers during DNA replication',
                    'It joins Okazaki fragments during DNA replication'
                ],
                'correct_answer' => 'It adds repetitive sequences to the ends of chromosomes to prevent loss of genetic information',
                'explanation' => 'Telomerase is an enzyme that adds repetitive sequences (TTAGGG in humans) to the ends of chromosomes (telomeres). This prevents the loss of genetic information during replication, as DNA polymerase cannot replicate the very end of a DNA strand (the end-replication problem).',
                'order' => 4
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'In a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, the frequency of a recessive allele a is 0.3. What is the frequency of the dominant phenotype?',
                'answer_type' => 'text',
                'correct_answer' => '0.91',
                'explanation' => 'If the frequency of allele a is q = 0.3, then the frequency of allele A is p = 1 - q = 1 - 0.3 = 0.7. The frequency of the recessive phenotype (aa) is q² = 0.3² = 0.09. Therefore, the frequency of the dominant phenotype (AA and Aa) is 1 - q² = 1 - 0.09 = 0.91.',
                'order' => 5
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'Describe the process of transcription in eukaryotes, including the role of RNA polymerase and transcription factors. Explain how post-transcriptional modifications affect the final mRNA product.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Transcription is the process by which the genetic information in DNA is copied into RNA. In eukaryotes, this process occurs in the nucleus and involves several steps.\n\nThe process begins with the binding of RNA polymerase II and various transcription factors to the promoter region of a gene. Transcription factors are proteins that help position RNA polymerase correctly and regulate when transcription occurs. They bind to specific sequences in the promoter region, such as the TATA box.\n\nOnce positioned, RNA polymerase II unwinds the DNA double helix at the transcription start site, creating a transcription bubble. It then moves along the template strand in the 3\'→5\' direction, synthesizing an RNA molecule in the 5\'→3\' direction according to the base-pairing rules (A pairs with U, G pairs with C).\n\nIn eukaryotes, the initial RNA transcript (pre-mRNA) undergoes several post-transcriptional modifications before leaving the nucleus:\n\n1. 5\' Capping: A modified guanine nucleotide is added to the 5\' end of the pre-mRNA. This cap protects the mRNA from degradation and helps in ribosome binding during translation.\n\n2. 3\' Polyadenylation: A poly(A) tail, consisting of multiple adenine nucleotides, is added to the 3\' end of the pre-mRNA. This tail also protects the mRNA from degradation and aids in export from the nucleus and translation.\n\n3. Splicing: Eukaryotic genes contain non-coding sequences called introns, which are removed, and coding sequences called exons, which are joined together. This process is catalyzed by spliceosomes, which are complexes of proteins and RNA molecules. Alternative splicing can produce different mRNA molecules from the same pre-mRNA, increasing the diversity of proteins that can be produced from a single gene.\n\nThese post-transcriptional modifications result in a mature mRNA molecule that is exported from the nucleus to the cytoplasm, where it can be translated into a protein. The modifications ensure the stability of the mRNA and regulate gene expression at the post-transcriptional level.',
                'order' => 6
            ]
        ]
    ],
    
