                'question_text' => 'Explain the process of photosynthesis, including the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light energy from the sun into chemical energy in the form of glucose.\n\nThe process occurs in specialized organelles called chloroplasts and can be divided into two stages: the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle.\n\nLight-dependent reactions:\n\n1. Light absorption: Light is absorbed by pigments such as chlorophyll and other accessory pigments in the thylakoid membrane.\n\n2. Excitation of electrons: The absorbed light energy excites electrons, which are then transferred to a special molecule called an electron acceptor.\n\n3. Transfer of electrons: The electrons are passed along a series of electron carriers in the thylakoid membrane, ultimately resulting in the formation of a proton gradient across the membrane.\n\n4. ATP synthesis: The proton gradient is used to produce ATP from ADP and Pi through the process of chemiosmosis.\n\nCalvin cycle:\n\n1. Carbon fixation: CO₂ is fixed into a three-carbon molecule called 3-phosphoglycerate (3-PGA) via the enzyme RuBisCO.\n\n2. Reduction: 3-PGA is reduced to form glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P) using the ATP and NADPH produced in the light-dependent reactions.\n\n3. Regeneration: The G3P molecules are used to regenerate the RuBP molecule, which is necessary for the cycle to continue.\n\nThe overall equation for photosynthesis is:\n\n6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) + 6 O₂',
                'order' => 2
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'What is the difference between a prokaryotic cell and a eukaryotic cell?',
                'options' => [
                    'Prokaryotic cells have a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells do not',
                    'Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have one',
                    'Prokaryotic cells are smaller than eukaryotic cells',
                    'Prokaryotic cells have a cell wall, while eukaryotic cells do not'
                ],
                'correct_answer' => 'Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus, while eukaryotic cells have one',
                'explanation' => 'Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, lack a true nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles. Eukaryotic cells, such as plants and animals, have a true nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles.',
                'order' => 3
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'Explain the process of meiosis, including the stages of prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Meiosis is the process by which sex cells (sperm and egg cells) are produced in eukaryotic organisms. It involves two consecutive cell divisions, meiosis I and meiosis II, which reduce the chromosome number by half and result in the production of four non-identical daughter cells.\n\nMeiosis I:\n\n1. Prophase I: Chromosomes condense and homologous pairs come together in a process called synapsis. Crossing over occurs, resulting in the exchange of genetic material between homologous chromosomes.\n\n2. Metaphase I: Homologous pairs of chromosomes line up at the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers.\n\n3. Anaphase I: Homologous pairs of chromosomes separate, moving to opposite poles of the cell.\n\n4. Telophase I: Nuclear envelope reforms around each set of chromosomes, and the cytoplasm divides.\n\nMeiosis II:\n\n1. Prophase II: Chromosomes condense again, and a new spindle forms.\n\n2. Metaphase II: Sister chromatids line up at the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers.\n\n3. Anaphase II: Sister chromatids separate, moving to opposite poles of the cell.\n\n4. Telophase II: Nuclear envelope reforms around each set of chromosomes, and the cytoplasm divides.\n\nThe result of meiosis is the production of four non-identical daughter cells, each with half the number of chromosomes as the parent cell.',
                'order' => 4
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'What is the function of the Golgi apparatus in a cell?',
                'options' => [
                    'Protein synthesis',
                    'Cell signaling',
                    'Protein modification and transport',
                    'DNA replication'
                ],
                'correct_answer' => 'Protein modification and transport',
                'explanation' => 'The Golgi apparatus is a complex organelle found in eukaryotic cells that is responsible for modifying, sorting, and packaging proteins and lipids for transport out of the cell or to other parts of the cell.',
                'order' => 5
            ],
            [
                'question_text' => 'Explain the process of natural selection, including the role of genetic variation, mutation, and gene flow.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Natural selection is the process by which populations of living organisms adapt and evolve over time in response to environmental pressures. It is a key mechanism of evolution and is based on the idea that individuals with certain traits or characteristics are better suited to their environment and are more likely to survive and reproduce.\n\nGenetic variation is the raw material for natural selection. It arises through mutation, gene flow, and genetic recombination during reproduction. Mutation is the process by which new genetic variants are created through errors during DNA replication or repair. Gene flow is the movement of genes from one population to another, which can introduce new genetic variants into a population.\n\nNatural selection acts on this genetic variation, favoring individuals with traits that are better suited to their environment. These individuals are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing their advantageous traits on to their offspring. Over time, this leads to the evolution of populations that are better adapted to their environment.\n\nThe process of natural selection can be summarized as follows:\n\n1. Genetic variation arises through mutation, gene flow, and genetic recombination.\n\n2. The environment acts on this genetic variation, favoring individuals with certain traits or characteristics.\n\n3. Individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing their traits on to their offspring.\n\n4. Over time, the population evolves to become better adapted to its environment.',
                'order' => 6
            ]
        ]
    ],
    
