                'question_text' => 'Explain the concept of acid-base chemistry, including the Arrhenius, Bronsted-Lowry, and Lewis definitions of acids and bases.',
                'question_type' => 'theory',
                'model_answer' => 'Acid-base chemistry is the study of the reactions between acids and bases. There are three main definitions of acids and bases: the Arrhenius, Bronsted-Lowry, and Lewis definitions.\n\nThe Arrhenius definition states that an acid is a substance that increases the concentration of hydrogen ions (H⁺) in a solution, while a base is a substance that increases the concentration of hydroxide ions (OH⁻).\n\nThe Bronsted-Lowry definition states that an acid is a substance that donates a proton (H⁺), while a base is a substance that accepts a proton.\n\nThe Lewis definition states that an acid is a substance that accepts a pair of electrons, while a base is a substance that donates a pair of electrons.\n\nThese definitions are not mutually exclusive, and many substances can be classified as acids or bases according to more than one definition.',
                'order' => 6
            ]
